course: dictionary
lesson: 15061
title: Revised: looked at again, made better
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To revise something means to look at it again and make it better. When you revise for a test, you go over what you learned. «I revised my spellings before the test.» Revised is the past form, so it tells you the revising has already happened.
explain: Revised also describes a thing that has been changed or updated. A revised plan is a plan that someone has changed, often to make it better. «The teacher handed out a revised timetable on Monday.» The timetable is not the same as the old one, because something was updated.
explain: Use the clues around the word. If the sentence mentions a test, a draft, a timetable or a price, revised probably means studied again or changed. Swap in «changed to make better» and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: To revise means to look at something again.
fact: «Revised» is the past form of revise, so it tells you about something that has already happened.
fact: «Revised» can also describe something that has been changed or updated, such as a revised edition of a book.
fact: «Carefully revised» means someone checked and changed something with great care.
fact: A good check is to swap in «studied again» or «changed to make better». If one of them fits, you have the right meaning.
exercise: «I revised for the spelling test last night.» What does revised mean here? | *Studied again | Ran away from | Lost completely | Drew a picture of
exercise: «The shop gave us a revised price.» What does revised suggest? | *Changed to a new price | Hidden under the counter | Free for everyone | The same as before
exercise: «The writer read the revised draft and smiled.» Find and copy the word that shows the draft was changed. | *revised | read | smiled | writer
test: «Her revised plan was much better than her first one.» What does revised mean? | *Changed to make it better | Lost in the post | Thrown away | Written in pencil
test: Which sentence uses revised to describe a thing? | *The book has a revised edition with new pictures. | I revised my notes all weekend. | We revised the rules after lunch. | Dad revised the garden fence.
exam: «After the first draft, the writer revised the story twice.» What does revised mean here? | *Changed and improved | Ignored | Read aloud | Sold
